From a2672ce0cdfd68e3b5725463c01f104da2178184 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: csolomon <charliesolomon@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 10:00:55 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] branded-cli: GAH_* environment names, and a --help page that
 describes this session

Two changes to the CLI surface, one patch because both touch printHelp()
in cli/args.ts and apply-patches.sh cannot recognise overlapping patches
as already applied.

== Environment names (#2) ==

0001-branding sets piConfig.name, and APP_NAME rebrands the banner, the
process title, the config directory and *_CODING_AGENT_DIR. A handful of
settings are still read from hardcoded PI_* names scattered across
coding-agent, tui and ai, so GAH_OFFLINE=1 did nothing while --help printed
PI_OFFLINE (#2).

Rather than patch every read site -- a hunk per file, in files upstream
touches often -- new file core/gah-env.ts mirrors each GAH_<NAME> onto
PI_<NAME> once, before anything reads them. Upstream keeps reading PI_*;
users write GAH_*. PI_* still works and wins if both are set, so nothing
already configured changes behaviour. Twenty-one inbound names are
covered: the five from the issue (OFFLINE, SKIP_VERSION_CHECK, TELEMETRY,
PACKAGE_DIR, CACHE_RETENTION) plus SHARE_VIEWER_URL and the tui/debug
knobs; the list is one array in that file.

The module is imported first from cli.ts and rpc-entry.ts, and its prefix
is a literal rather than derived from APP_NAME, for a bootstrap reason:
PI_PACKAGE_DIR is consulted while config.ts locates package.json, before
the branded name exists, and PI_TIMING is read at module load. The SDK
entry point does not pass through it; SDK consumers set PI_*.

Names the agent exports to child processes (PI_CODING_AGENT, and
PI_SESSION_ID / PI_MODEL / ... in the bash tool's environment) keep
upstream's spelling on purpose: skill scripts written against upstream's
documentation must keep working.

cli/args.ts: the help text documents the environment under the branded
prefix (${APP_NAME.toUpperCase()}_...), padded so the columns still align.

Verified with the built binary: --help lists GAH_PACKAGE_DIR, GAH_OFFLINE,
GAH_TELEMETRY, GAH_SHARE_VIEWER_URL and no PI_ names; GAH_PACKAGE_DIR=
/nonexistent makes --version fall back to 0.0.0 exactly as PI_PACKAGE_DIR
does, and setting both with PI_ pointing at the real package restores
0.84.4. test/gah-env.test.ts covers the mirroring rules.

== The help page (#32) ==

Upstream's help is one large template in cli/args.ts describing everything
pi can do: a package manager, credential printers, thirty provider API
keys, and flags the GAH policy makes inert. For the people GAH targets it
is mostly false -- it says bash is on and grep is off, that the default
provider is google, that PI_OFFLINE exists -- and args.ts changes every
release, so correcting it in place would be a recurring sync cost.

New file cli/gah-help.ts renders a short page from what is true in this
process: the environment the launcher set (GAH_BUILTIN_MODELS,
GAH_ALLOWED_HOSTS, GAH_PROVIDERS_FILE, GAH_SKILLS_DIR, GAH_AUDIT_LOG), the
tool allowlist the policy pack enforces (policy.ts exports it as
GAH_EFFECTIVE_TOOLS at extension load, and extensions load before help
prints), and the extension flags that registered. It lists only the
options and GAH_* variables that mean something under policy, names the
AWS variables only when Bedrock is allowlisted, shows no provider API
keys, and frames GAH as a harness rather than a coding assistant.

printHelp() delegates with a one-line hunk; `--help --verbose` falls
through to upstream's full reference. test/gah-help.test.ts covers the
renderer on a private environment.

Re-authored 2026-09-02 (#35): the help names powershell as the Windows
shell tool next to bash. Comment-only.

