Re-authored 2026-09-08 (#45, #52: sync to v0.85.1): cli.ts is now three
lines around setupCli(); the gah-env import keeps its place as the first
import. Upstream moved the experimental process roles to a source-only
entrypoint in 0.85.1, so no dispatcher hoist is needed here.

Re-authored 2026-09-17 (#100): the Usage block lists the scaffolding
subcommands the launcher publishes in GAH_SCAFFOLD_COMMANDS rather than a
hardcoded `init`. Folded in here rather than added as 0003 for the reason
this patch already gives: gah-help.ts is created by this patch, so a
later patch editing it stops `git apply --reverse --check` recognising
this one as already applied, and apply-patches.sh then reports both as
broken on every clean checkout. Tried it; that is exactly what CI said.

Why it was needed: the launcher handles the subcommand and never passes
the token on, so nothing in this process could notice when init-kb,
update-kb and update-skills were added to the launchers, and nothing
failed while the page fell three behind. The page also advertised `init`
in packaged deployments, where the launcher refuses every scaffolding
subcommand because the templates they copy are not shipped there.

Names come from the launcher and wording from a table in the renderer, so
a subcommand this file has never heard of still appears under a generic
description. scripts/check-skills.sh asserts the launchers agree on the
list, that every name has wording, and that the launchers which cannot
reach a subcommand clear the variable rather than leaving it unset.

Re-authored 2026-10-09 (#142): the page could not be reached on the
shared host. People there have no shell, their login shell drops
arguments, and gah-launch forwarded none. So the policy pack shows the
same "This session" rows in a session, as /help. The rows move into
gahSessionRows(), exported from the package index (one line in
index.ts), because an extension can import only what the index exports.
The command and this page call the same function, so they cannot
disagree.

The Skills row counts skills with pi's own loader (loadSkillsFromDir):
the shared host passes each skill folder with its own --skill, so the
row listed every path; it now reads "<folder> (N skills)". It also
counts the folder a skills fetch adds during a session
(GAH_SESSION_SKILLS_DIR), and says when a path is missing or holds no
skills.

With GAH_LAUNCHER_KIND=host the page is those rows and where to go next:
no usage, options, examples or scaffolding subcommands, which nobody on
the host can pass. gah-launch execs bin/gah, which publishes the
scaffolding list, so clearing the variable in gah-launch never took
effect; scripts/check-skills.sh asserts the host page end to end
instead, and scripts/check-help.sh that /help and --help agree.
